            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Mandarin for to give up all hope, then the Mandarin for may your wishes all come true."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: nìngkě",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "nìngkě — \"would rather\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "wǒ nìngkě zǒulù yě bù xiǎng děng chē — I'd rather walk than wait for the bus."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Its characters come later; for now it is a word for the ear."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "nìngkě is built from nìng and 可 'may', both inherited from Middle Chinese, neng and khaX (Guangyun, in Baxter's transcription). The dictionary glosses the first 'calm' and, after an older dictionary, as a word of wishing. The falling tone of nìng is not the regular outcome of neng, which gives níng."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for saying what would happen."
            }
          ]
        },
